Body:
Confirmed against the actual workflow source, and there's an exact precedent: #49122 hit this identical false positive and was merged by @Hona once explained.

`pr-standards.yml` runs on `pull_request_target`, which always executes the copy on `dev` (the default branch) regardless of a PR's actual base branch. `v2`'s copy of that file already has a fallback for this, with a comment explaining why:

```js
// GitHub only populates closingIssuesReferences when a PR targets the repository's
// default branch (dev). PRs targeting other branches like v2 always return totalCount 0.
// Fall back to checking the PR description for closing keywords (e.g. Closes #123).
const body = pr.body || '';
const issueMatch = body.match(/### Issue for this PR\s*\n([\s\S]*?)(?=###|$)/);
const issueContent = issueMatch ? issueMatch[1].trim() : body;
const hasBodyIssueRef = /(closes|fixes|resolves)\s+#\d+/i.test(issueContent) || /#\d+/.test(issueContent);
```

`dev`'s copy doesn't have that fallback yet, so it only checks `closingIssuesReferences` (always `0` here) and flags every `v2`-targeted PR, regardless of the description.

This PR's body already matches that fallback's expected format (`Closes #49743` under `### Issue for this PR`), so it'd pass today if the same fallback were ported from `v2` to `dev`.
